<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Gampola</h1><p class="st-meta">1341 CE – 1412 CE (approx.) · capital Gampola</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Sinhalese kingdom of the central hills, under which Buddhist temple architecture flourished and the Alakeshvara family rose to power.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuvanaikabahu-iv">Bhuvanaikabahu IV</a> moved the royal seat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurunegala">Kurunegala</a> to Gampola, on the Mahaweli river in the central highlands, around 1341. The move followed the drift of Sinhalese power away from the northern plains after the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-dambadeniya">Kingdom of Dambadeniya</a> and the pressure of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jaffna-kingdom">Jaffna Kingdom</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Gampola kings were weak, and real power lay with ministers, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alagakkonara-family">Alakeshvara</a> family, who built a fortress at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kotte">Kotte</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a> and resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna">Jaffna's</a> expansion. The period produced fine Buddhist monuments, notably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadaladeniya-vihara">Gadaladeniya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lankatilaka-vihara">Lankatilaka</a> temples, which blend Sinhalese and south Indian styles. Contacts with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> increased.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1411 the Chinese admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a> fought and captured the ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vira-alakeshvara">Vira Alakeshvara</a> and took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Soon afterwards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parakramabahu-vi">Parakramabahu VI</a> took power and moved the capital to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kotte">Kotte</a>, founding the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-jayawardenepura-kotte">Kotte</a> in 1412.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-gampola.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Gampola" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Original: Sri Lanka Vectorization: Zscout370, Mike Rohsopht · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sri_Lanka.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1341 CE – 1412 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gampola</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bhuvanaikabahu IV, Vikramabahu III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sinhala</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Sri Lanka</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gampola</dd></dl></aside></div>
